=== 1. the two branches on Z/5 ===
   e = x/2      : [0, 3, 1, 4, 2]   (x -> 3x)
   o = (3x+1)/2 : [3, 2, 1, 0, 4]   (x -> 3 - x)
   [ok] e(x) = 3x mod 5 (order 4, fixes 0)
   [ok] o(x) = 3 - x mod 5 (a reflection of the additive pentagon, fixes -1 = 4)
   [ok] e sends additive pentagon edges {i,i+1} to pentagram edges {i,i+2}
   [ok] e sends pentagram edges {i,i+2} to pentagon edges
   [ok] o preserves pentagon edges
   [ok] <e, o> = AGL(1,5), order 20
   [ok] <e^2, o> = D_5 (order 10): e^2 = (x -> -x) and o are two mirrors of the additive pentagon

=== 2. spectrum and eigenvectors of A = 2K_5 (exact) ===
   charpoly(2K_5) = x^5 - 2x^4 - 2x^3 + 5x^2 - 2x = x*(x - 2)*(x - 1)*(x**2 + x - 1)
   [ok] charpoly(2K_5) = x (x-1) (x-2) (x^2+x-1)
   eigenvalues of K_5: 1, 1/2, 0, (sqrt5-1)/4 = cos 72 = 1/(2 phi), -(sqrt5+1)/4 = -cos 36 = -phi/2
   [ok] cos(72 deg) = 1/(2 phi) and cos(36 deg) = phi/2 (sympy exact)
   [ok] right eigenvector for 2K-eigenvalue 2: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
   [ok] right eigenvector for 2K-eigenvalue 1: [-1, 0, 0, 0, 1]
   [ok] right eigenvector for 2K-eigenvalue 0: [1, 0, 1, -1, -1]
   [ok] left eigenvector (measure) for 2K-eigenvalue 2: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
   [ok] left eigenvector (measure) for 2K-eigenvalue 1: [-2, 2, 1, 0, -1]
   [ok] left eigenvector (measure) for 2K-eigenvalue 0: [1, -1, 0, -1, 1]
   [ok] right golden eigenvector f = (phi^4, 1, 2phi, -phi^2, -2phi^3):  2K f = f/phi
   [ok] f = a + phi*b with a = (2,1,0,-1,-2) (sawtooth = -centred residue of x + 1/2), b = (3,0,2,-1,-4)
   [ok] on the golden lattice W = Za + Zb:  2K a = b - a,  2K b = a   (matrix [[-1,1],[1,0]] = inverse Fibonacci matrix up to GL2(Z))
   [ok] (2K)^2 a + 2K a - a = 0  (the sawtooth is a cyclic vector of the golden block)
   [ok] W mod 5 = the affine-linear functions F_5 -> F_5 (a = 2 - x, b = 2x + 3 mod 5)

=== 3. the folding: cells  Y = {-1/4} = {1},  Z = {+-1/2} = {2,3},  X = {0,-1} = {0,4} ===
   [ok] Y, Z, X partition Z/5
   push-forward of 1_Y: {3: Fraction(1, 1), 2: Fraction(1, 1)}   1_Z: {1: Fraction(2, 1), 4: Fraction(1, 1), 0: Fraction(1, 1)}   1_X: {0: Fraction(1, 1), 3: Fraction(1, 1), 2: Fraction(1, 1), 4: Fraction(1, 1)}
   [ok] push(1_Y) = 1_Z
   [ok] push(1_Z) = 2*1_Y + 1_X
   [ok] push(1_X) = 1_X + 1_Z
   matrix of 2K^T on span(1_Y, 1_Z, 1_X) (columns = images): [[0, 2, 0], [1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 1]]
   [ok] its charpoly is (x-2)(x^2+x-1)
   [ok] the same 3x3 matrix is the pentagon adjacency restricted to mirror-symmetric functions (cells apex / near / far)
   [ok] 2K^T = adjacency of the 5-cycle (1, 2, 0, 4, 3) on span(1_Y,1_Z,1_X); steps [1, 3, 4, 4, 3]; is an affine image of the additive pentagon: False
   [ok] 2K^T = adjacency of the 5-cycle (1, 2, 4, 0, 3) on span(1_Y,1_Z,1_X); steps [1, 2, 1, 3, 3]; is an affine image of the additive pentagon: False

=== 4. the same statement on integers: T^k(n) mod 5 for n = 1 (mod 5) is a pentagon random walk ===
   k =  0: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [0, 1, 0, 0, 0]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [0, 1, 0, 0, 0])  return prob 1 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  1: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [0, 0, 1, 1, 0]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [0, 0, 1, 1, 0])  return prob 0 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  2: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [1, 2, 0, 0, 1]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [1, 2, 0, 0, 1])  return prob 1/2 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  3: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [1, 0, 3, 3, 1]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [1, 0, 3, 3, 1])  return prob 0 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  4: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [4, 6, 1, 1, 4]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [4, 6, 1, 1, 4])  return prob 3/8 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  5: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [5, 2, 10, 10, 5]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [5, 2, 10, 10, 5])  return prob 1/16 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  6: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [15, 20, 7, 7, 15]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [15, 20, 7, 7, 15])  return prob 5/16 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  7: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [22, 14, 35, 35, 22]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [22, 14, 35, 35, 22])  return prob 7/64 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   k =  8: counts of T^k(n) mod 5 = [57, 70, 36, 36, 57]  (2^k x pentagon-walk law in residue order: [57, 70, 36, 36, 57])  return prob 35/128 = (2^k + 2(-1)^k L_k)/(5 2^k)
   [ok] for k = 0..14: law of T^k(n) mod 5 given n = 1 mod 5 (all 2^k residues mod 5*2^k) = law of the simple random walk on a pentagon after k steps
   [ok] spectral formula for the walk law at k = 12: 1/5 + (2/5)(cos^k72 cos(72j) + cos^k144 cos(144j))

